{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 8,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.69,
            0.046,
            0.863,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Square and A Cube"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.087,
            0.849,
            0.146
          ],
          "content": "3. We know that a perfect square is obtained by multiplying an integer by itself. Will looking at a number’s prime factorisation help in determining whether it is a perfect square?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.149,
            0.848,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": "Yes, if we can divide the prime factors of a number into two equal groups, then the product of the prime factors in either group combine to form the square root."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.215,
            0.388,
            0.237
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Is 324 a perfect square?"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.24,
            0.608,
            0.258
          ],
          "content": "3 2 4 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 .",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.258,
            0.421,
            0.278
          ],
          "content": "These can be grouped as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.282,
            0.635,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}324 &= (2 \\times 3 \\times 3) \\times (2 \\times 3 \\times 3) . \\\\&= (2 \\times 3 \\times 3)^2 = 18^2 .\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.325,
            0.685,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": "We can also write the prime factors in pairs. That is,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.349,
            0.65,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "3 2 4 = ( 2 \\times 2 ) \\times ( 3 \\times 3 ) \\times ( 3 \\times 3 ) ,",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.373,
            0.638,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": "which shows that 324 is a perfect square. Thus,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.396,
            0.558,
            0.416
          ],
          "content": "324 = (2 \\times 3 \\times 3)^{2} = 18^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.239,
            0.418,
            0.449,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, $\\sqrt{324} = 18.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.439,
            0.388,
            0.463
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Is 156 a perfect square?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.464,
            0.602,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": "The prime factorisation of 156 is $2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 13.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.484,
            0.471,
            0.503
          ],
          "content": "We cannot pair up these factors."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.502,
            0.524,
            0.521
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, 156 is not a perfect square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.523,
            0.846,
            0.547
          ],
          "content": "？ Find whether 1156 and 2800 are perfect squares using prime factorisation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.548,
            0.848,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": "We can estimate the square root of larger perfect squares by looking at the closest perfect squares we are familiar with and then narrowing down the interval to search."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.607,
            0.674,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": "For example, to find 1936 , we can reason as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.633,
            0.837,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": "(i) 1936 is between 1600 (402) and $2 5 0 0 \\: ( 5 0 ^ { 2 } )$ , so $40 < \\sqrt{1936} < 50.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.658,
            0.848,
            0.696
          ],
          "content": "(ii) The last digit of 1936 is 6. So, the last digit of the square root must either be 4 or 6. It can be 44 or 46."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.193,
            0.7,
            0.848,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "(iii) If we calculate $4 5 ^ { 2 } ;$ we can compare it with 1936 to halve the interval to search from 40–50 to either 40–45 or 45–50. We can write $4 5 ^ { 2 }$ as $(40 + 5)(40 + 5) = 40^2 + 2 \\times 40 \\times 5 + 5^2$ $1600+400+25=2025$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.781,
            0.56,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": "(iv) 2025 > 1936. So, $40 < \\sqrt{1936} < 45$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.807,
            0.847,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "(v) From the observation in point b we can guess and then verify that $\\sqrt { 1 9 3 6 }$ is 44."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.854,
            0.503,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": "Consider the following situations —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.873,
            0.848,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": "Aribam and Bijou play a game. One says a number and the other replies with its square root. Aribam starts. He says 25, and Bijou quickly"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.884,
            0.915,
            0.905,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": "9"
        }
      ]
    }
